es riff and then  two minutes in it explodes into this  frantic garage rock jam he said how this  shift in the music made him visualize an  entire car chase film scene and then  years later in 2011 after Wright made a  few movies that were successful he  directed a music video for mint Royale  called blue song and he said that that  vision in his head transformed into this  concept of a getaway driver waiting  while heist goes down rocking out alone  in a car to the beat of the music with  the song used as a timer for the heist  he actually planned for this music video  to be the opening scene of his eventual  film baby driver and six years later  that's pretty much what he did the first  two minutes of dell bottoms is the timer  for buddy darling and Griff who robbed  the bank and then when they return the  music kick arrives and the chase begins  with each tire screech and police siren  all set to the beat of the music now  this synchronization might have reminded  you of the trailers for Suicide Squad  the way gunfire and punches all  correspond to Bohemian Rhapsody  however right actually said that this is  even more of a challenge in Suicide  Squad trailers work because instead of  an editor just matching up shots to  music in an editing Bay after the fact  he did this process on the set in the  direction of the actors and the stunt  drivers and yeah this is way harder but  right did say it also saved money  because storyboarding each sequence two  sections of a song force them to think  more mathematically because then they're  zero-fat when you're shooting it like  the song won't get any longer so the  stunts have to be exact and stay within  budget okay now let's move on to the  sequence that comes immediately after a  three-minute tracking shot following  Beatty on his morning walk to get coffee  sit to the music of Harlem shuffle by  Bob and Earl  [Music]  they may have noticed that this song  starts the same way as House of Pain  jump around  nothing is how the pain actually sampled  this exact riff from Harlem shuffle and  Wright said that he intentionally wanted  to catch us off-guard like this and he  does it throughout the film using more  obscure covers of songs while in other  cases switching back to the lesser-known  original version Wright also got British  DJ mark Nicholson also known as Ossie me  so to compose a new mix of Harlem  shuffle - whoo blend in audio from  people on the street footsteps people  talking dogs barking and ATM beeping as  woman punches in her pin even the actor  Ansel Elgort saying yeah yeah - the beat  of the music really have to watch this  movie again because even the words and  the signs and the graffiti on the walls  sync up to words that you hear in the  song at that moment the point of all  this is to put us firmly inside baby's  head car accident that he suffered as a  kid left him with a constant rain sound  so he's constantly listening to music to  drown it out so when we go inside his  head we see that the world around him is  alive with music it's as if he's  composing all of these sounds into his  own personal life soundtrack which is  something he literally does later in  this movie now of course this theme was  carefully choreographed ahead of time  Wright brought in the choreographer Ryan  Heffington and he said he walked through  the sequence with an iPhone camera over  and over again to get it precise the end  result is this amazing sequence that  looks like it could be from a musical  like lala land okay let's move on to the  next big action sequence the second  heist in the truck and the music here is  neatly neat by the Damned  [Music]  [Applause]  now Edgar right since the script of the  actors along with the thumb drive  containing the soundtrack and flee from  the Red Hot Chili Peppers who plays  Eddie no nose in the scene solve this  song neatly need in the tracklist and  that is what sealed the deal for him to  be in the film and the fun thing about  the song is that it's too short only  about 2 minutes long so when they were  storyboarding the scene the  cinematographer of the movie bill Pope  told right that they were going to run  out of song these trucks just couldn't  move as fast as right wouldn't need them  to to fit within the song so right said  he came up with a creative solution he  added this shot where they have to  change cards and baby refuses to hit the  gas until he resets the song wait I got  to start the song over Ok Go and this is  great because it also tells us more  about baby as a character he is that  obsessed with having music playing all  the time even if it slows him down for a  second and again this is disciplined by  Edgar Wright insisted so hard on having  this music that he crafted the story and  the character around it and it led to  this great character revealing moment  now we could probably look at the music  in every scene and baby driver like this  like even baby meeting Deborah and the  diner is structured around Carla  Thomas's ba D Y and T Rexes Deborah and  Becks Deborah enter I would love to dig  into those but let's stick with the  action and fast-forward to this  warehouse shootout the music here is  tequila but instead of the popular  champs version that everyone knows right  again use a cover version this one by  the button-down brass now the middle of  this song features a dueling drum solo  and when right first heard that he said  that it would be amazing to do a  gunfight scene to that and because there  was so much gunfire in the scene it  drowned out all the music playing from  the speakers on set or in the earbuds so  the choreographer and stunt guy had to  teach each actor the rhythm of the shot  and then as they repeatedly shot the  scene they had to keep counting them in  like 5 6 7 8 bang bang bang and Wright  said that on one take when he was  checking in with Jamie Foxx to make sure  he had the gunfire rhythm down Fox was  walking him through it saying this guy  goes up about and I go bah bah and then  he jokingly mimed taking a drag on a  cigarette and right love that invisible  cigarette so much that he had Fox do it  for the movie so yeah this is on  another example of this meticulous  choreography to the music  leading to a really fun memorable image  that right probably what I've had in the  film otherwise okay let's take a look at  one last great example of Wright's piece  of music and baby driver later in the  movie the foot chase the music here it's  focused by hocus-pocus god I love saying  [Music]  now Wright said that this was his  favorite part of the movie but that the  studio actually wanted to cut it they  refused to pay for the scene but he  wanted it seemed so bad that he paid out  of pocket for two extra days of shooting  the film now Wright said that he first  heard hocus-pocus on a music show in  England and that he was blown away by  this progressive rock song that unlike  most songs changes tempo throughout the  song now like most songs just have one  tempo that plays all the way through  which isn't really helpful for film  scoring since the action changes so fast  but Wright described focus as the  perfect cardio track that dictates where  baby goes like fast guitars for when  he's sprinting and then this yodeling  section for when he's hiding behind a  tree and then another shift when he's  running through the mall and then an  accordion section when he breaks into a  car now these were just the big examples  of creative music editing that stood out  to me but what was your favorite musical  moment baby drivers also are there any  other movies or TV shows that you think  would have benefited from this approach  of letting music they take the story  really there is so much going on in baby  driver and I would love to do a full  breakdown of all the miserable stuff  that I caught but really just more  people need to go see this movie and you  can help get the word out by sharing  this video  liking i